PrimeOracle: the median, the deviation bound, and the composite mark
==========================================================================

2. one dishonest publisher
   panels swept, sizes 3..7:          18340
   landed outside the adjacent pair:  0
   Read: on any panel of three or more, replacing one answer with
   anything at all, including the top of uint128, leaves the median
   between the honest answers on either side of the middle. It moves
   by one order statistic and no further.

   The same sweep against the mean, which is the arrangement not
   chosen: one liar drags it up to 113427455640312821154458202477256070484 above the honest maximum.
   That is the whole argument for the median in one number.

3. f dishonest publishers: does the median leave the honest range?
   n\f   0  1  2  3  4  5  6  7
   1     -  X  .  .  .  .  .  .
   2     -  X  X  .  .  .  .  .
   3     -  -  X  X  .  .  .  .
   4     -  -  X  X  X  .  .  .
   5     -  -  -  X  X  X  .  .
   6     -  -  -  X  X  X  X  .
   7     -  -  -  -  X  X  X  X
   '-' inside the honest range, 'X' outside it.
   The boundary is f >= n/2 at every n, which is what makes a strict
   majority the right quorum: it is the largest f the median still
   survives. DeployVenue._quorum is seats/2 + 1 for that reason.

4. the composite mark
   price                                10000000000 / rate 8109665    -> 123309655824
     exact 123309655824.254150, floored, so the loss is at most one tinybar
   price                                          1 / rate 3          -> 33333333
     exact 33333333.333333, floored, so the loss is at most one tinybar
   The floor moves the mark down. A lower mark is a position closer
   to a margin call, which is toward the lender and away from the
   party the call is taken against. Stated in OracleMath's header.

5. wrote 72 panels, 7 deviation cases and 6 marks
   -> ../venue/test/fixtures/median.json
   venue/test/PrimeOracle.t.sol replays every one of them.
